acmesh bundles third-party code. This file records whose it is and under which
licence it is distributed, so that a binary leaving the house carries the
notices it must carry.

The full licence texts are in the module cache after a build
($GOPATH/pkg/mod/...) and in the vendor trees of the projects themselves. What
follows is the notice, not a replacement for the text.

1. u-root
   Source:  https://github.com/u-root/u-root
   Pinned:  869f00f6b224ce02b1db51806159d6cd9c246f76
   Licence: BSD-3-Clause
   Used as: the commands in this binary, either linked from u-root directly or
            forked into internal/forked (see internal/forked/manifest.json).
   Copyright (c) The u-root Authors.

2. gobusybox (part of u-root, separate module)
   Source:  https://github.com/u-root/gobusybox
   Pinned:  v0.6.0
   Licence: BSD-3-Clause
   Used as: the build tool (makebb) and the dispatcher around the command
            table.
   Copyright (c) The u-root Authors.

3. mvdan/sh
   Source:  https://github.com/mvdan/sh
   Pinned:  v3.14.1
   Licence: BSD-3-Clause
   Used as: the shell behind "sh" and "gosh".
   Copyright (c) 2016, Daniel Martí.

4. Go standard library and golang.org/x/sys
   Licence: BSD-3-Clause (the Go authors)
   Used as: everything underneath.

What a distributed binary has to carry: the three notices above with their
copyright lines, and the licence texts. A build that ships acmesh without them
is not distributable, which is why they live in the repository next to the code
that uses them rather than in a tarball nobody reads.
